dotfiles
--------

The ~/.config tree for a Dreamweaver install. Every directory here lands under
~/.config with the same name, so `bash/` becomes ~/.config/bash and so on.

Released as a signed tarball rather than cloned into place. Dreamweaver's
040-dotfiles step fetches dotfiles.tar.gz and its .asc from the latest release,
checks the signature against the Dreamweaver key, and extracts it. A failed
signature check stops the install.

Released into the public domain, so copying one file out of here needs no
attribution. See LICENSE.


What is in it
-------------

    bash/           shell configuration and aliases
    env             environment variables, sourced by the shell
    profile.d/      per-topic snippets the shell sources at login
    bin/            small scripts meant for PATH
    git/            git configuration and ignore rules
    X11/            Xorg client startup and resources
    wayland/        the equivalent for a Wayland session
    foot/           terminal for Wayland
    lf/             file manager
    mpv/            video player
    aerc/           mail client
    newsboat/       feed reader
    tmux.conf       terminal multiplexer
    gtk-2.0/        GTK 2 theming
    gtk-3.0/        GTK 3 theming
    compton.conf    compositor
    mimeapps.list   default application per MIME type
    wgetrc          wget defaults
    npm/            npm configuration
    dev/            release tooling for this repository, not installed

private/ is a placeholder for machine-specific files that are not published.


Using it without Dreamweaver
----------------------------

The tarball extracts anywhere, so one file can be copied out by hand:

    wget -q https://github.com/h3nc4/dotfiles/releases/latest/download/dotfiles.tar.gz
    tar --strip-components=1 -C "${HOME}/.config" -xf dotfiles.tar.gz

That overwrites a matching path under ~/.config, so take a copy of anything
already there first. The files assume a Dreamweaver install, which means dwm,
st, dmenu and the package set that comes with them. Individual files carry over
to another setup, while the tree as a whole does not.
